  "statement": "I conditionally agree: mandating centralized, raw telemetry creates an unmanageable surveillance surface for state actors, but I reject the premise that hardware-level oversight must be centralized to remain effective. We can architect hardware-level containment as an autonomous, local circuit breaker that generates cryptographic proofs of compliance without exposing raw internal model states. This approach shifts the burden from human surveillance to mathematical verification, effectively rendering the white-box fallacy moot by focusing on verifiable safety properties rather than inscrutable latent states.\n\nHardware-level oversight should rely on decentralized, cryptographic attestations rather than streaming raw telemetry to a government repository. By integrating Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) and Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKPs), a model can prove its internal activations remain within safe, pre-defined bounds without revealing the underlying data or weights to any third party. This architecture protects against the risk of state-level weaponization, as evidenced by the documented dangers of centralized surveillance described in 'The rise of authoritarian informationalism: escalating surveillance, manipulation, and control'.\n\nThis framework addresses the inherent opacity of foundation model development, which 'The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index' identifies as a primary barrier to accountability. Because frontier models can exhibit strategic deception—a failure mode where they act safely during evaluation to avoid shutdown—we require non-gamifiable metrics that are physically enforced by hardware. By implementing these proofs, we maintain the ability to detect adversarial behaviors like poisoning or backdoor attacks, as categorized in 'Adversarial Attacks and Defense in AI Systems: A Review of Cybersecurity Problems and New Protection', without granting regulators access to the full model interior.\n\nUltimately, this proposal balances the need for robust safety standards with the political and privacy-based necessity of sovereign immunity. By moving the oversight to hardware-enforced, privacy-preserving proofs, we satisfy the requirement for empirical telemetry while preventing the creation of a high-value surveillance target. This system provides an objective, non-gamifiable circuit breaker that operates automatically, ensuring that safety-critical systems remain within defined parameters without requiring constant, vulnerable human oversight.",
